.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt ADJTIMEX 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm adjtimex ,
.Nm clock_adjtime
.Nd read or adjust the real-time clock's NTP state
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/timex.h
.Ft int
.Fn adjtimex "struct timex *buf"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_adjtime "clockid_t clock" "struct timex *buf"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn adjtimex
reads the state the real-time clock keeps for NTP into
.Fa buf ,
after making the changes the bits of
.Fa buf->modes
ask for, and returns the clock's state:
.Dv TIME_ERROR
while
.Dv STA_UNSYNC
is set, as it is from boot until a program clears it, and
.Dv TIME_OK
otherwise.
.Fn clock_adjtime
does the same for
.Fa clock ,
which must be
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME .
.Pp
The modes are Linux's, and any but
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET_SS_READ
needs a privileged caller.
The clock keeps Linux's NTP state and slews real time by it, as Linux 6.12
does: at each second the next second's ticks take the length that the tick
and the frequency give, plus a share of the offset left and of
.Fn adjtime Ns 's
slew, and real time runs at that rate from where it is, so that it neither
jumps nor goes back.
.Bl -tag -width ADJ_OFFSET_SINGLESHOT
.It Dv ADJ_OFFSET
With
.Dv STA_PLL
set, the offset, at most half a second either way, goes to the
phase-locked loop: from the next second it slews 2^-(2 +
.Fa constant )
of what is left each second, and it changes the frequency by the offset
times the seconds since the last offset, at most 2^(3 +
.Fa constant ) .
With
.Dv STA_FLL
set and 256 seconds or more since the last offset, or with over 2048
seconds since it, the frequency-locked loop's change, the offset over
those seconds, is added too, and
.Dv STA_MODE
is set.
.Dv STA_FREQHOLD
keeps the frequency.
.Fa offset
reads what is left.
.It Dv ADJ_FREQUENCY
The frequency, in units of 2^-16 ppm, at most 500 ppm either way, which
changes the rate at once and which settings of the clock keep.
.It Dv ADJ_TIMECONST
The loop's time constant, from 0 to 10, plus 4 without
.Dv STA_NANO .
.It Dv ADJ_TICK
The length of a tick of USER_HZ, from 9000 to 11000 microseconds; 10000 is
the timer's own rate, whatever the timer's frequency, and another length
runs real time faster or slower by its ratio.
.It Dv ADJ_OFFSET_SINGLESHOT
.Fn adjtime Ns 's
offset, in microseconds, which musl and glibc pass in this mode: from the
next second it is slewed at 500 microseconds a second, and
.Fa offset
returns what was left of the one before.
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET_SS_READ
returns what is left and changes nothing.
.It Dv ADJ_SETOFFSET
Steps real time by
.Fa time ,
as
.Xr clock_settime 2
does.
.El
.Pp
A step, by
.Dv ADJ_SETOFFSET ,
.Xr clock_settime 2
or
.Xr settimeofday 2 ,
forgets the offset and
.Fn adjtime Ns 's
slew in progress, keeps the frequency and sets
.Dv STA_UNSYNC ;
it counts the timers and sleeps of real time again and cancels a timerfd
set with
.Dv TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET .
A slew cancels nothing, and each change of its rate counts the timers and
sleeps of real time again at the new rate.
.Dv ADJ_MAXERROR ,
.Dv ADJ_ESTERROR ,
.Dv ADJ_TAI
and
.Dv ADJ_STATUS
are kept and read back; the maximum error grows by 500 microseconds a
second, and
.Dv STA_UNSYNC
is set once it passes 16 seconds.
.Dv ADJ_NANO
and
.Dv ADJ_MICRO
choose the unit of
.Fa offset
and of
.Fa time.tv_usec .
The precision is 1 microsecond and the tolerance Linux's 500 ppm; the
fields of PPS read 0.
.Pp
While
.Dv STA_UNSYNC
is clear, a privileged call that changes anything also copies the time,
rounded to the second, to the CMOS clock, at most once in 11 minutes, as
Linux's 11-minute mode does, so that the next boot starts from the time
an NTP daemon kept.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 124 for
.Fn adjtimex
and 343 for
.Fn clock_adjtime ,
which take the 128-byte
.Vt struct timex
of 32-bit fields, and 405 for
.Fn clock_adjtime64 ,
which takes the 208-byte
.Vt struct __kernel_timex
of 64-bit fields that musl and glibc use.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm adjtimex
is call 159 and
.Nm clock_adjtime
305, and their
.Vt struct timex
has the x86-64 layout, its longs 64 bits; there is no time64 form.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The clock's state, or -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
BusyBox's
.Xr adjtimex 1
prints the state, and its
.Xr ntpd 1
sets the clock through these calls and
.Xr clock_settime 2 .
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
reads the state and requires
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
of
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa buf
is not readable and writable.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Dv ADJ_TICK
asks for a tick outside 9000 to 11000 microseconds,
.Dv ADJ_FREQUENCY
for a frequency whose product by 65536000, its scaled form, does not fit
64 bits,
.Dv ADJ_SETOFFSET
for a fraction not under a second or a time before the time since boot or
past the year 2232, the bit of
.Fn adjtime Ns 's
modes, 0x8000, is given without
.Dv ADJ_OFFSET ,
or
.Fa clock
is not a clock.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa clock
is a clock other than
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME .
.It Bq Er EPERM
A mode other than reading was asked for by a caller without privilege.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock_settime 2 ,
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
.Xr timerfd 2
.Sh BUGS
Linux slews
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
with real time; here it keeps the timer's rate, so the two part by the
slew.
.Dv STA_INS
and
.Dv STA_DEL
are kept, and no leap second is inserted or deleted.
There is no PPS signal.
